Date: Fri, 8 Jan 93 17:45:49 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Is our list really damaged?

What follows are some personal comments about this list, about what
may or may not be broken or damaged.

I'm sure you've all seen signs of damage--the flames, the mini-flames,
the polarization, the folks departing the list (apparently, based on
their non-presence, though I have no way of knowing who and how many
have formally unsubscribed).

(And I'm not casting stones while living in a glass house--I was
culpable in some of my posts during the pre-Xmas "troubles," culpable
to the extent of deciding to take a few weeks off from posting to
reflect on things.)

I've been reading this list since early July, 1992, and posting to it
fairly frequently (and at length!) since later that month. I've seen a
couple of generations of posters come and go (or at least I assume
they're gone, as I no longer see their names...). Mostly I've enjoyed
the list, but these past few months the problems have begun to really
get to me.

Here are just some of the problems I see...others may wish to add to
the list or comment as they see fit (of course). By the way, some of
them I mentioned in my satirical "cmprssd pstng" a few days ago (and,
ironically, I got e-mail from folks who thought I was being frivolous,
was making fun of the List members, etc...as they say on the Net,
"Jeesh!").

1. Polarization is growing. Maybe it's always been there and I've just
become more attuned to it. But it seems that the anarchocapitalists
are at war with the humanists (pardon the labels, but I don't want to
name names, to avoid hard feelings or charges of personal attacks),
the ocean colonizers are at war with the computer types, the
pagan-Asatru (sp?) folks are at war with the atheists, the yogic
teleporters are at war with.....

2. This polarization shows up in odd ways. When I posted a postive
reaction to David Jevans's essay, I got a negative reaction from
others I'm usually more aligned with (sort of a "We thought your were
one of us...I guess we misjudged you" kind of reaction).

3. I notice several posters are feeling the need to state their "bona
fides" to the list in the hopes of fending off flames about their
politics. (I know I've been doing it recently...) This may have to do
with our "lack of history" and the fact that few of us have personal
connections with others on the List (those of us in the Bay Area are
luckier than most in this respect--and after the party tomorrow with
Harry S., even more of us will know each other). This is a well-known
aspect of e-mail societies.

Also, words on a screen carry more weight than words casually spoken
in conversation. We see people taking offense at what, in
conversation, would be mostly ignored and quickly forgotten. But once
in print, the comments live on!

4. As I alluded to in my "cmprssd pstng," the list may already be too
big. Imagine a party with hundreds of people listening in on all
conversations and prepared to jump in whenever they disagree with
_any_ part of what's being said! Newcomers may challenge the basics,
oldtimers may be dismissive of such challenges, and so forth. A recipe
for constant tension.

Now with a real party, conversation groups will naturally form and
various folks will leave to avoid conflict, to find more interesting
(and higher bandwidth) conversations, and just to wander off in random
directions. But what we've got here on this list is almost an "all or
nothing" party, as if everybody was sitting in a circle--a giant
circle of hundreds of people.

Not surprisingly, there are disagreements about the topics discussed,
about the "hogging" of conversation by some, and so on. (However,
lists like this one allow posting by anyone, so nobody is excluded. A
pure market, of sorts.)

The goals of Max More's "Extropy" is presumably to boost circulation,
for various and obvious reasons. But if the List expands to more than
the current base (hundreds? with a few dozen active posters?), things
will get much worse!

We may see the expansion of kill file usage, with all the negative
effects of that. More positively (pun intended), we may see the
development of "positive reputation" systems, which have been
discussed recently at length on the Cypherpunks mailing list, notably
by Dean Tribble (who, sadly, chose to withdraw from _this_ list
because of the volume, which sort of makes my point).

Eventually we may have "rooms" in cyberspace, a la MUDS and IRC. Using
the "party" metaphor, folks will be free to move into a room and thus
reduce the bandwidth problem.

Splitting the list up is a possibility. In fact, it's already
happening--the Ex-Digest, Ex-Essay, Ex-Bay, Ex-Boston, etc., lists are
already sublists, obviously. There are many negatives to this
splitting, discussed by several posters in the post-Xmas debate on
whether the list is broken or not, and I'm not necessarily
recommending it.

5. And now for a different kind of concern: I think we're running out
of topics to discuss! (This could be _me_, of course, but I sense this
is the view of others as well, even if unspoken...correct me if I'm
wrong on this one.)

We've discussed--several times--uploading and consciousness, ethics of
AIs, nanotech, PPL, personal fitness, smart drugs, the Fermi paradox,
pseudoscience, cryptography, basic Extropian principles, favorite
songs and books, and on and on and on. And this is just since last
summer--I assume real old-timers have seen these topics thrashed even
more times! Gag me with a carbyne rod!

Absent new events, new developments, how many more times can these
"golden oldies" be debated? Granted, newcomers are all enthusiastic
about debating the "basics," but many of us are not. Perhaps this is
why we are dismissive (though some brave souls, like Perry M., have
seemingly inexhaustible energies to debate the basics) and why we
don't spend the careful time attempting to selll our vision to
newcomers and skeptics (a point made by Tom Morrow to Betsy Schwartz,
explaining the way we Extropians and libertarians often fail to
"soften our views" (these are the words of an ex-girlfriend of mine,
not Tom's, but it was the same point) to better persuade the dubious.

(By the way, is is because so much energy has already been expended on
these topics, these "golden oldies," that I favor opening up the
archives of past postings. My solution, which I've posted about
before, is to have those who want their postings made freely available
to simply say so...perhaps in their .sig, or perhaps in a separately
compiled list. Those who don't want their posts made available should
not be affected (there _is_ the problem of quoting parts of posts, but
this is solvable and should not affect the main scheme). It's a damn
shame that so many posts are apparently gone forever, either lost
without a trace or blocked from reposting. Ironically, Perry told me
this whole issue was debated last spring---but I can't see the debate
because I wasn't on the List back then...what a hoot!)

So is the list broken?

Maybe not, but it may be time to give it a major overhaul.

Thank you for reading this far, and for your contributions. 

Cheers!

-Tim

