Date: Sat, 9 Jan 1993 00:20:42 -0500 (EST)
From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.UU.NET (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Re: Libertarian Citations Request

>   The concept and extent of property and protection related to intellectual
>   property, particularly notions of trade secret, copyright, and patent.
> 
>   Concepts of protection/stewardship of common (?) resources such as air
>   and water supplies.

You picked two doozies.  These and the rights of children and/or individuals
not mentally competent (for reasons of congenital defect, injury, etc.) are
*the* toughest issues facing libertarian ethical philosophers.  They generate
a correspondingly high level of controversy among librtarians.

Libertarians are divided between a majority of those (like myself) who support
strong (though not necessarily government-enforced) intellectual property
rights, and a minority that believe that information should not (or, in
some theories, *cannot*) be property.  The minority position is more popular
among hackers than elsewhere, largely due to FSF's influence.

The libertarian position on "stewardship" is that it basically only works when
the "stewards" have good selfish reasons to preserve value.  Thus, we look for
property-based and market-based solutions.  There are fierce debates, however,
over the proper handling of third-party effects (how to externalize costs) and
the "free rider" problem.

