It hit me last night that a race of thinking talking beings could reach the
Stone Age without the ability to count.  (Hardly an earth-shaking
revelation, since there are stone-age human tribes who lack numbers.)  Maybe
they have so many tentacles (which break off and re-grow) that the question
"how many tentacles do they have" is of dubious meaningfulness, so they
never learn to count on their fingers.  So maybe there are people Out There
who lack mathematics.  Such people could still prevent the emergence of a
second tool-using species on their planet.  Perhaps the ability to do math
is rare, and that's why we're alone.

> Perhaps conciousness will never improve.  I can image computer systems so
> different from today, with hand-waving to "program" them, self-aware
> computers, completely graphical VR computers, with the entire earth and
> every individual networked together.  Yet we are stumped by the halting
> problem, and 90 million people still use DOS! 

Do they really?

We're stumped by the halting problem like we're stumped by finding a
rational expression of the square root of 2:  it can't be done.  It's easy
to show why it can't be done.

Of course, all mathematical proofs rest on the assumption that if there were
a flaw, human minds would detect it.  What if there were some kinds of
logical flaw that, because of the structure of our minds, we cannot detect?
A fallacy to which we are mind-blind?  A logical analog to visual illusions?

Our vision works in a particular way, and there are evolutionary reasons for
the way it works.  The "flaw" that makes us see a triangle where there isn't
one, to take James Burke's example, may well have been adaptive in the
trees.  Similarly, we have evolved logical structures for drawing inferences
from what appears on the surface to be inadequate information
(underdetermined problems).  We know that these sometimes lead us astray (in
judging risk, for example).  When we do logic, are we able to build a mental
"virtual logic machine" that excludes all such flaws?

> 8.  Perhaps a free market system results in catastrophe.  Oil tanker
> spills. Nuclear accident.  Pollution.  AIDS or other fatal virus.  There
> are so many disaster scenarios that a free market system makes possible,
