Does statism make disasters any less likely?  I think not, because
regulators don't pay for their mistakes.

> "Let the market decide".  That pretty much admits that
> humans are incapable of planning, doesn't it! 

No, it admits that other humans don't have the same plans I have.  Nobody
can plan for all the goals of everyone else, and no one authority can have
all the knowledge that various people use to make their plans.  

> Once you have global effects from individuals screwing up, you can't just
> let the market decide.  Some Bozo is gonna natural select himself, and
> take us all with him.

I'd say that's a good reason to avoid concentrating power.  Someone on this
list recently asked, "Who the f*ck put a nuclear armada in the hands of an
80-year old ex-actor?"  Who built the Mutual Assured Destruction machines in
the first place?  Not the marketplace, and not a nut in a garage -- indeed
not anyone spending his own money.

> 9.  Have any of you had sex recently?  Gone for a hike in the woods?
> Visited an art gallery or attended a concert?  Ridden a motorcycle up
> the Oregon coast?  Read some Eastern philosophy?

Amazingly enough, I've done none of those things since summer.  Spouse left
me; it's not easy for me to get to the woods (no car); I dislike concerts;
family pressure has kept me from learning to ride a motorcycle; and when
Spouse left she took her books on Zen (not that I read them, but I did
discuss Zen with her sometimes).

I'm not far from the caricature who spends all his free time at the
computer.

> . . . .  Pure extropianism is as narrow minded a view as any other.  Since
