Date: Sat, 9 Jan 93 08:45:16 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re:  Missing Gray Goo explained

A few people have expressed the idea that it might be good to convert
solar system resources into computers which run a virtual reality
system that we could all upload and live in.

I'm wondering whether this is the best use of these resources.  Consider
just the earth.  Our choices are to live on the surface of the planet,
perhaps with improvements due to nanotech and such, or to destroy the
surface, convert it to a giant computer network which _simulates_ the
surface of the planet, and live there.  This need not be an ecological
catastrophe because we can also upload and simulate animals and plants,
as well as weather and such.

Now, the VR has obvious advantages because it is more flexible.  But I do
think there are questions as to whether a certain volume of computers can
simulate as much virtual space as the physical space they take up.  If
the simulation is at an atomic level of detail, the computers would seem
to take up more space than the VR they are providing.  It's only by
reducing the resolution of the simulation that we can provide a VR with
adequate volume.

So, there is going to be a tradeoff between "quality" (i.e. resolution)
of the VR and available room.  It also seems like a physical reality
which runs at full atomic resolution might provide more variety and
surprises than a VR which is more planned and runs at less resolution.

Another advantage a VR has is that you wouldn't be restricted to just the
surface of the earth as raw materials for the computers.  Theoretically
the whole earth could be converted, giving you a vastly greater volume of
computers which should allow a correspondingly larger VR to be run.
Comparing the volumes of planets in the solar system to their surfaces
makes it more likely that converting everything to VR's would give more
living room.

But, this ignores the possibility of physically making use of planetary
interiors through tunnelling and such.  There is a classic science
fiction story, "Ker-Plop", about a spaceship the size of a small moon
which has levels upon levels upon levels, about 100 feet apart, with
lighting, farming and such at each level.  This spaceship was capable of
providing living room for more people than all the earth-like planetary
surfaces in the galaxy (in the story).

If this were done to the earth, again I suspect that larger and
higher-quality living quarters could be provided than by converting the
whole earth into computers and running a VR for everyone to live in.

To use the whole solar system like this, we would either have to change
the temperatures and such of the other planets, or use engineering to
allow ourselves and other life forms to live there.  Both Clifford
Simak's classic "City" and Marc Stiegler's nanotech short story, "The
Gentle Seduction" (perhaps in an homage to Simak), describe the
pleasures of life on Jupiter.  A hollowed-out Jupiter with millions of
internal levels would be even bigger in terms of living room.

Hal Finney

